  "design": {
    "decision": "Bid your [K]s as likely winners and lead them early; your own [A]s are the only ones left, so bid more aggressively.",
    "opponent": "Very visible: their [A]s become [2]s before they bid, so they bid lower and must find tricks elsewhere. It is a deliberate defensive piece against a runaway leader.",
    "rationale": "The brief favors defense and comebacks in a lopsided game, and this is the strongest visible defensive hand change; the uncommon uc-opp-twos is random and weaker. Departures: it reveals one fact about the opponents' hands (they hold no [A]s), and it can feel bad to the other team; both are accepted to test whether a strong suppression piece reduces runaway leads.",
    "partners": [
      "ca-king-hold",
      "ua-king-two-x",
      "cb-opp-set-mult",
      "ub-opp-set-xmult"
    ],
    "cluster": "C",
    "draftText": "Opening: Every [A] the opponents hold becomes a [2]",
    "hooks": "Text template for an Opening 'become' that has both whose=opponents and a from filter ('Opening: Every [A] the opponents hold becomes a [2]'); the kernel already applies whose to BecomeFrom, so only text and simplicity need it.",
    "critic": {
      "verdict": "keep",
      "concerns": "It hard-counters an opponent's whole Ranks build (bought [A]s and become-[A] enablers) with no way to respond, and it reveals that no other [A]s are in play (an information departure the designer accepts).",
      "elegance": 3
    }
  },
